<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceolred of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 716</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceolred was king of Mercia from 709 to 716, the son of Æthelred. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Woden</a>'s Barrow in 715. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> condemned him for his immoral life and for violating the privileges of monasteries, and he is said to have died in a fit of madness at a feast. Æthelbald, whom he had driven into exile, succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ceolred-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Ceolred of Mercia" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Margaret Dovaston. In a book by Walter Hutchinson, published by Hutchinson &amp;amp; Co. LTD. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceolred_of_Mercia_Dovaston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
